+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Narrows NG-9
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared searching for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian out of scope — only owners are searchable. Like the rest of the Non-Goals table, it was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, and its provenance stayed open: whether the row was deliberately declined, deferred, or never considered was unknown.
+
+A request has now arrived to filter the veterinarian directory by specialty. That request forces a boundary decision: NG-9 as written would refuse it, yet filtering by an attribute the directory already displays is a narrower capability than the free-text search NG-9 was concerned with.
+
+The owner has decided the boundary (2026-08-23).
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Refuse under NG-9 as written.** Rejected: NG-9's rationale is that repeating owner-style free-text search elsewhere adds surface without teaching a new pattern. Filtering an already-shown attribute is a different, narrower capability, so the rationale does not reach it.
+2. **Retire NG-9 entirely and open veterinarian search.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian search still adds surface without a new pattern. Only the specialty filter is wanted.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** (chosen). Filtering the directory by a specialty it already shows is in scope; free-text veterinarian search stays out.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed, not retired. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays is in scope, delivered as REQ-VET-003 (browser page) and REQ-VET-004 (machine-readable list). Free-text search for a veterinarian, a pet, or a visit remains a non-goal.
+
+The machine-readable veterinarian list is reinstated as a supported surface; the specialty filter is its first requested capability. The withdrawn REQ-VET-002 id is not reused. In this round the filter is a request-address contract only — no form, dropdown, or other page control is added; a visible control may come as a follow-up request.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open provenance consultation as a narrowed row; its remaining scope (free-text search) is now a deliberate boundary, not a survey artifact.
+- The machine-readable list carries a requirement again after being withdrawn to the Superseded list. Its route is no longer pending removal.
+- The filter is matched on the whole specialty name, case-insensitively; a partial value matches nothing and an unmatched specialty returns an empty list rather than an error.
+- Adding a visible page control for the filter later is a follow-up request, scoped on its own.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row and preamble.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — REQ-VET-003 and REQ-VET-004.
+- [PRD Superseded](../prd.md#superseded) — the reinstatement note against REQ-VET-002.
